# Git commit and push — user edits message first

When the user asks to commit, push, or “commit and push”:

1. Inspect changes (`git status`, `git diff`, recent `git log`) and draft a **proposed commit title** (first line) and **description** (body, optional).
2. **Stop before `git commit` or `git push`.** Present the proposal in chat:

   ```text
   Proposed commit title:
   <one line>

   Proposed description:
   <body or “(none)”>
   ```

3. Ask the user to **edit or approve** the title and description. Use their final wording exactly in `git commit`.
4. Only after explicit approval (or they paste their revised title/body), run `git add`, `git commit`, and `git push` if they asked for push.
5. Do not amend or push unless they request it. Do not skip hooks or change git config.

If they approve commit but not push, commit only. If they change the message after seeing the proposal, use the updated text without arguing.
